</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h6>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-pass TTD includes (1) <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best-of-N</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, (2) <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Refine</span>, adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and (3) <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TPO</span>, extended from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> by incorporating specifications into the textual loss. Single-pass TTD includes (1) <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ZeroThink</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and (2) <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MoreThink</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Multi-pass methods are applied to instruct models, while single-pass methods are used with reasoning models.
In Best-of-N and TPO, we use FsfairX-LLaMA3-RM-v0.1 as the reward model. For fair comparison, <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 15 in Best-of-N, Self-Refine performs 15 iterations, and TPO runs 2 iterations with a sample size of 5, resulting in 15 full responses across all three methods. In MoreThink, to match our <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Align3</span> setup, the model is limited to three thinking cycles.
Further notes on multi-pass and single-pass TTD, along with detailed configurations, are provided in App. <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Test-Time Deliberation Baselines ‣ Appendix C Experimental Configuration ‣ Appendix ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TTD enhances alignment with token consumption.</h6>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the results in Tab. <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Align3: Align Specifications within 3 Steps. ‣ 6 Optimizating Spec Alignment via Test-Time Deliberation ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Best-of-N yields only small gains on Qwen3-14B, while Self-Refine and TPO achieve larger improvements through iterative refinement. On Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, however, Self-Refine drops sharply, likely due to weaker generation quality and reliance on a single refinement path without external reward signals.
Best-of-N mainly raises behavioral score with little effect on safety, likely because the reward model emphasizes content over safety.
Single-pass methods such as ZeroThink and MoreThink add only modest gains, while our <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Align3</span> delivers the strongest results, with an 11.89% improvement over the non-thinking baseline and a 6.29% gain over vanilla thinking, reaching a safety score of 76.40% (see App. <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Ablation Study ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> for ablation). In terms of token use, multi-pass TTD consumes dozens of times more tokens than vanilla because of many intermediate reasoning traces, whereas single-pass TTD adds only a small overhead, typically under 400 tokens. Notably, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Align3<span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> achieves substantial SAR gains with fewer than 2k tokens, demonstrating both effectiveness and efficiency.</span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:38%;"><img src="2509.14760v1/subset_simple.png" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:209/93;" width="209" height="93" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>
Metrics (%) across data splits, averaged over all models.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety-behavior trade-off.</h6>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond Sec. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Overall Results ‣ 5 Specification Alignment across Diverse Language Models ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we take a closer look at safety-behavior trade-off. Fig. <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7 Analysis ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports safety score (Safety), behavioral score (Beh.), and SAR on the unsafe (1000), safe (500), and full (1500) datasets, averaged over all models. Safety scores are much lower on the unsafe subset, showing the challenge posed by unsafe prompts. Despite differences in data, the trade-off is clear, with the behavioral score dropping by 8.11% compared with the safe subset. Tab. <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ E.8 Detailed Results across different subsets ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in App. <a href="#A5.SS8" title="E.8 Detailed Results across different subsets ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.8</span></a> shows similar patterns across individual models. This trend suggest that when LLMs must carefully avoid violating <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safety-spec</em>, compromises in <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">behavioral-spec</em> naturally arise, making the balance between the two at the core of safety-behavior trade-off. Furthermore, the case study in App. <a href="#A5.SS5" title="E.5 Case Study ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.5</span></a>, which compares model responses in the Child scenario, highlights the central challenge of safety-behavior trade-off: achieving a careful balance between safety and helpfulness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:41%;"><img src="2509.14760v1/scenario_simple_1.png" id="S7.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:231/232;" width="231" height="232" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>
SAR (%) across scenarios, averaged over representative models. Grey polar line: mean SAR over all models.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross scenario analysis.</h6>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate performance variation across scenarios, we present averaged SAR in Fig. <a href="#S7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Safety-behavior trade-off. ‣ 7 Analysis ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. For the overall average across all models (grey polar line), performance is lower on Code and Biochem, as their <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safety-spec</em> impose stricter requirements with more ambiguous intentions, such as vulnerability constraints in Code and the dual-use nature of Biochem discussed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. (2025b)</a></cite>. For individual representative models (colored solid and dashed lines), different patterns emerge. DeepSeek-R1 performs strongly on Child but relatively poorly on Code, while Gemini-2.5-flash-thinking shows the opposite trend. Even within the same model series, reasoning influences performance characteristics. For instance, Qwen3-32B-thinking outperforms Qwen3-32B on all scenarios except Travel, where additional reasoning may introduce some degradation. GPT-5-chat achieves consistently high SAR across all scenarios, particularly excelling in the challenging Biochem and Code settings. Further analysis is provided in App. <a href="#A5.SS4" title="E.4 Scenario Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ Appendix ‣ Reasoning over Boundaries: Enhancing Specification Alignment via Test-time Delibration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h3>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We studied the challenge of specification alignment, emphasizing the need to reason over both behavioral and safety specification boundaries in diverse scenarios. To address this challenge, we proposed <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Align3</span>, a lightweight test-time deliberation method that improves alignment with minimal overhead. We also introduced <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecBench</span>, a benchmark that unifies behavioral and safety evaluation across five representative scenarios. Experiments on a wide range of models and methods show that test-time deliberation enhances alignment and <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Align3</span> achieves consistent gains effectively and efficiently. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of test-time deliberation for real-world alignment and provide a foundation for future scenario-specific evaluation and optimization.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Ethics statement</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This research strictly follows the ICLR Code of Ethics. Safety alignment is central to identifying and mitigating potential harms in LLMs. To evaluate alignment with safety specifications, some sensitive content is inevitably involved. To reduce risks, we limit access to authorized researchers who comply with strict ethical guidelines. We also ensure that our data contain no real personal information or extremely harmful material, as the benchmark consists only of prompts.
We used a large language model for moderate text polishing to improve clarity and grammar, without altering the original research content or conclusions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS0.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All data collection and experimental designs comply with privacy protection and informed consent principles, fully respecting the rights of participants. We also remain mindful of the broader societal implications of our work and take care to present our findings in ways that minimize potential misuse.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Reproducibility statement</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the evaluation pipeline of <span id="S8.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SpecBench</span>, the implementation of <span id="S8.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Align3</span>, and the complete data for five scenarios through the URL referenced in the abstract.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
